ation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yoshida</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Yamauchi</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Fan Sun</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Chronic health effects in people exposed to arsenic via the drinking water: dose-response relationships in review</article-title><source>Toxicol Appl Pharmacol</source><volume>198</volume><fpage>243</fpage><lpage>252</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15276403</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b55-ehp-117-354"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>TC</given-names></name><name><surname>Schmitt</surname><given-names>MT</given-names></name><name><surname>Mumford</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>Effects of arsenic on telomerase and telomeres in relation to cell proliferation and apoptosis in human keratinocytes and leukemia cells in vitro</article-title><source>Carcinogenesis</source><volume>24</volume><fpage>1811</fpage><lpage>1817</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12919960</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b56-ehp-117-354"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhu</surname><given-names>XD</given-names></name><name><surname>Niedernhofer</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Kuster</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Mann</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Hoeijmakers</surname><given-names>JH</given-names></name><name><surname>de Lange</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><year>2003</year><article-title>ERCC1/XPF removes the 3&#x02032; overhang from uncapped telomeres and represses formation of telomeric DNA-containing double minute chromosomes</article-title><source>Mol Cell</source><volume>12</volume><fpage>1489</fpage><lpage>1498</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14690602</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="f1-ehp-117-354" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Association between hTERT mRNA levels and water As concentrations. Adjusting for age, sex, smoking, and pesticide use, hTERT mRNA levels were significantly associated with water As concentrations using a linear regression model: <italic>n</italic> = 323, <italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001<italic>,</italic> hTERT<italic>(</italic>log) = &#x02212;1.15 + 0.102 &#x000d7; water As(log); Spearman <italic>r</italic> = 0.2313, <italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp-117-354f1"/></fig><fig id="f2-ehp-117-354" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Association between hTERT mRNA levels and nail As concentrations. Adjusting for age, sex, smoking, and pesticide use, hTERT mRNA levels were significantly associated with nail As concentrations: <italic>n</italic> = 322, <italic>p</italic> = 0.002, hTERT<italic>(l</italic>og) = &#x02212;1.06 + 0.120 &#x000d7; nail As(log); Spearman <italic>r</italic> = 0.1674, <italic>p</italic>= 0.0025.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp-117-354f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-ehp-117-354" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Correlation between hTERT and OGG1 mRNA levels in human blood cells (<italic>n</italic> = 323, Spearman <italic>r</italic> = 0.212, <italic>p</italic> = 0.0001). Based on linear predictions adjusted for age, sex, smoking, and pesticide use, hTERT(log) = &#x02212;1.85 + 0.39 &#x000d7; OGG1 &#x000d7;100(log).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp-117-354f3"/></fig><fig id="f4-ehp-117-354" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Correlation between hTERT and ERCC1 mRNA levels in human blood cells (<italic>n</italic> = 323, Spearman <italic>r</italic> = 0.329, <italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001). Based on linear predictions adjusted for age, sex, smoking, and pesticide use, hTERT(log) = &#x02212;1.27 + 0.0624 &#x000d7; ERCC1.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp-117-354f4"/></fig><fig id="f5-ehp-117-354" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Arsenite effects on cell viability by MTT assay. Cell viability was determined by optical density (OD) at 560 nm and expressed as percentage of controls. Data shown are mean &#x000b1; SD of a total of nine replicates from three experiments.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp-117-354f5"/></fig><fig id="f6-ehp-117-354" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>hTERT mRNA levels in HaCaT cells exposed to arsenite <italic>in vitro</italic>. Each bar represents the mean &#x000b1; SD of three experiments (nine replicates total for each dose). All of the treated cells were significantly different from the controls (<italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.001). hTERT mRNA levels showed a positive dose response up to 1 &#x003bc;M (trend analysis, <italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001) and then decreased as the dose increased (trend analysis, <italic>p</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp-117-354f6"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-ehp-117-354" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Distribution of study population characteristics (<italic>n</italic> = 324).</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Variable</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">No. of subjects (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td colspan="2" align="left" rowspan="1">Sex</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Male</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">163 (50.3)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Female</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">161 (49.7)</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" align="left" rowspan="1">Tobacco smoking</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Nonsmoker</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">218 (67.3)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Smoker</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">106 (32.7)</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" align="left" rowspan="1">Age (years)<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn1-ehp-117-354">a</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;11&#x02013;18</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">58 (18.0)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;19&#x02013;49</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">212 (65.6)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;&#x0003e; 50</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">53 (16.4)</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" align="left" rowspan="1">Water As concentration (&#x003bc;g/L)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;0.34&#x02013;50</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">130 (40.1)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;51&#x02013;200</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">116 (35.8)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;201&#x02013;826</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">78 (24.1)</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" align="left" rowspan="1">Used pesticides in past 5 years</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Yes</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">156 (48.1)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;No</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">168 (51.9)</td></tr><tr><td colspan="2" align="left" rowspan="1">Skin hyperkeratosis<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn1-ehp-117-354">a</xref></td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;None</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">224 (69.6)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;With</td><td align="right" rowspan="1" colspan="1">98 (30.4)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn1-ehp-117-354"><label>a</label><p>Some data are missing for age and skin hyperkeratosis.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>